# Overview
#
# A chatbot phrase frames text as a chat reply and says nothing about
# the subject (`feel free to open an issue`, `don't hesitate to ask`).
# The reader gains nothing from it. The fix gives the instruction (`open
# an issue`) or states the option (`you can open an issue`). The rule
# sits at Recommended, because human-written READMEs and contributing
# guides use these phrases to invite a reader.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Required ProseChatbot holds every other chatbot phrase. This tier
# holds the phrases that human-written READMEs also use. `I would be
# happy to help` draws a finding from each tier, because the Required
# tier reports `I would be happy to` and this tier reports `happy to
# help`.
#
# ProseIfWhether leaves `know` out of its verb list, so only this rule
# reports `let me know if`. ProseNegativeContraction reports the `don't`
# of `don't hesitate to`, so that phrase draws a finding from each rule,
# and no guard separates the two.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 15 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 15 of 15 sampled
# hits invited the reader.
expr main [(feel free to), (happy to help), (let me know if), ([don't, (do not)] hesitate to)]
let message "Chatbot phrase. Delete it, and give the instruction or the option directly."
let description "Flags `feel free to`, `happy to help`, `let me know if`, and `don't hesitate to`, which frame text as a chat reply."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Open an issue for any bug you find."
